.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timberfs
is a FUSE filesystem purpose-built for log files. Files in the mount look
and behave like ordinary log files \(em loggers append,
.BR tail (1)
and
.BR grep (1)
work unmodified \(em but the data is stored in a backing directory as
chunks compressed with zstd, together with a per-chunk
.B write-time index
that records when each chunk was written. Asking \(lqwhat was written
between 13:42 and 13:43?\(rq is a binary search plus a few frame
decompressions, independent of file size.
.PP
Files are append-only: writes anywhere but the end of the file fail with
.BR EPERM .
Truncating a file to zero is allowed and starts it over
(copytruncate-style rotation); rename and unlink work normally.
.B ls \-l
shows the logical (uncompressed) size while
.B du
shows the real, compressed disk usage.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS mount
.B timberfs mount
.I BACKING MOUNTPOINT
serves the logical view of backing directory
.I BACKING
on
.IR MOUNTPOINT ,
in the foreground. Unmount with
.B fusermount3 \-u
.IR MOUNTPOINT .
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-size " BYTES"
Uncompressed buffer size that triggers a chunk flush (default 262144).
.TP
.BI \-\-level " N"
zstd compression level (default 3).
.TP
.BI \-\-flush\-age " SECONDS"
Maximum time appended data may sit unflushed (default 5). Bounds both the
time granularity of the index and the data lost in a crash without
.BR fsync (2).
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Let other users access the mount. Unprivileged users need
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ;
root (e.g. the systemd unit) does not.
.SS create
.B timberfs create
.I DEST
makes an empty log with its properties declared up front in a
.B .bark
manifest \(em one flat, optional, human-editable JSON object beside the
pair.
.B \-\-index
declares the token index, database-style: once declared, EVERY writer
maintains the
.B .grain
automatically \(em
.BR import ,
the appender, the mount and both network intakes alike \(em extending it
incrementally for new chunks, trimming it by the same prefix when
retention drops chunks off the head, and rebuilding it if it went missing
(say, dropped by a rotation), so there is no per-write flag to forget. A streaming writer indexes on its once-a-second tick, so the
grain trails the newest chunk by up to that tick; an uncovered chunk is
scanned, never missed.
.B \-\-wal
declares the write-ahead sidecar
.RB ( .sap ):
every streaming writer fsyncs it once a second, shrinking a crash's data
loss from
.B \-\-flush\-age
down to that tick, at the cost of writing appended bytes twice (raw to
the sidecar, compressed into the eventual chunk). It buys visibility with
the same file:
.B "query \-\-follow"
tails its live edge, so entries reach a follower as they are appended
rather than a flushed chunk at a time. A property of the
store, like
.BR \-\-index :
declared once, every later writer honors it with no flag.
.B \-\-if\-not\-exists
makes an existing store a success instead of an error \(em CREATE IF NOT
EXISTS, for provisioning that re-runs on every start. The store is left
exactly as it is: nothing is written, and a declaration its manifest
disagrees with is WARNED about rather than applied \(em
.B set
changes a store that already exists. Without the flag an existing store
is an error, which is what a one-shot create wants.
.B \-\-set
.I KEY=VALUE
records free-form provenance
.RB ( host ,
.BR path ,
.BR format ,
\|.\|.\|.). Every manifest is minted with a durable identity on first write:
.B id
(a random UUID, constant across renames, moves and hosts \(em the
identity of the store, where paths are merely its current address) and
.B created
(RFC3339). Artifacts made by
.B export
and by rotation into a new segment are NEW stores: they get a fresh
identity, a
.B derived_from
pointer to the source's id (lineage chains compose across re-carves and
shipping), a
.B derived_op
of
.BR export / rotate
(copy vs move \(em whether the source still holds the data), and inherit
the data-provenance fields; settings
.RB ( index )
do not inherit. Rotation mints its source's identity when missing (it
holds the writer locks); export never writes its source. Content facts
\(em actual spans, sizes \(em are deliberately NOT recorded in bark: the
artifact's own rings state them authoritatively. The REQUESTED window is
different \(em an operation fact, like
.B derived_op
\(em and a windowed export records it as
.BR window_from / window_to
(RFC3339 UTC; only the bounds given; never inherited). Content can never
state coverage \(em an artifact whose last line is 17:00 doesn't say
whether 17:00\(en24:00 was covered-but-silent or not covered \(em and for
an EMPTY artifact the declared window is its entire meaning. Unlike the
derived
.BR .grain ,
bark survives head-drops, travels on rename, and ships inside
.B .timber
bundles.
.TP
.B \-\-retain\-unconsumed
Declare the third retention axis: keep what this store's RETAINING
followers have not read, on TOP of the age and size axes rather than
instead of them. Requires
.BR \-\-retain\-size ,
since interest only ever holds MORE and one stalled follower would
otherwise fill the disk. See
.B RETENTION
and
.BR FOLLOWERS .
.SS set
.B timberfs set
.I FILE KEY=VALUE\|.\|.\|.
declares or changes a store's properties in its
.B .bark
manifest \(em validated and atomic, which hand-editing the JSON is
neither. Known settings are parse-checked
.RB ( retain =90d,
.BR retain_size =50G,
.BR index =true|false,
.BR wal =true|false,
.BR cursors =/var/lib/timberfs
.RI ( "an absolute directory, SUPERSEDED by the follower registry \(em see"
.BR FOLLOWERS ));
.BR timestamp_regex ,
.B timestamp_format
and
.B timestamp_utc
are parse-checked too (see THE TWO CLOCKS); anything else is free-form
provenance
.RB ( host =...),
of which
.B service
is a convention rather than a rule \(em
.BR timber\-otlp (1)
reads it as the OTLP
.B service.name
and
.B otlp\-intake
seeds it on the stores it creates.
.B \-\-unset
.I KEY
removes a key. Identity and lineage
.RB ( id ,
.BR created ,
.BR derived_* )
are facts, not settings, and are refused. Works on LIVE stores: writers
re-read the manifest on their once-a-second retention tick, so
.B timberfs set backing/app.log retain=30d
takes effect within a second with no restart \(em restarting a writer
means restarting whatever pipes into it. A manifest that stops parsing
mid-flight (a bad hand-edit) keeps the writer alive on its last good
policy, with one warning: never silently unbounded, never a dead
producer.
.B wal
is the one exception: it is read once, when a writer opens the store, so
.B timberfs set backing/app.log wal=true
takes effect for the
.I next
writer, not one already running.
.SS append
.B timberfs append \-\-into
.I DEST
reads standard input and appends it to a log in the backing store directly,
with no FUSE mount involved \(em the
.BR svlogd (8)/s6-log
pattern (destinations are always named
.BR \-\-into ,
here as everywhere):
.PP
.nf
.RS
myapp 2>&1 | timberfs append \-\-into logs\-backing/app.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
With
.B \-\-records
standard input is a
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream instead of raw text: entries arrive pre-framed, and an entry
carrying its original write window
.RB ( wf / wl )
keeps it \(em write history survives the pipe. Without a carried
window, append stamps now, as always \(em the stream's word is law,
silence falls back to the command's own clock. Delivery is streaming:
data lands as it arrives, and a truncated stream keeps what came and
fails the exit code (at-least-once \(em re-running may duplicate; use
.B import \-\-records
for all-or-nothing).
.PP
Each log has exactly one writer (an exclusive per-file lock), but
appenders for different files happily share a backing directory; a
directory served by a
.B timberfs mount
is unavailable to appenders and vice versa. Appending to an existing log
continues it.
.PP
A supervisor typically starts the replacement writer BEFORE the one it
replaces has exited \(em Apache spawns a new piped\-log program on reload
while the old one is still draining its pipe \(em so a held lock is
normally a handoff in progress, not a conflict. An appender therefore
waits
.B \-\-wait\-for\-writer
seconds (default 5) for the lock, which makes such a restart silent
instead of one failure per log per reload; nothing is read from standard
input while waiting, so the producer's pipe buffers \(em which is why the
wait stays short.
.B \-\-wait\-for\-writer 0
fails on the first attempt. A lock still held at the deadline is
reported with its holder \(em writers record themselves in the
.B .lock
file, so the message names the process and its command line (checked
against /proc, never repeated as fact for a pid that has gone), which is
what separates a stuck handoff from a writer leaked by something else.
.B timberfs info
names it too.
.PP
Data is flushed into chunks by the same size and age rules
as the mount; end of input,
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGINT
flush and sync everything before exit. Accepts the same
.BR \-\-chunk\-size ,
.B \-\-level
and
.B \-\-flush\-age
options as
.BR mount ,
plus:
.TP
.BI \-\-wait\-for\-writer " SECS"
Seconds to wait for a departing writer to release the log before failing
(default 5; 0 = fail immediately). See above.
.TP
.B \-\-wal
Declare the write-ahead sidecar
.RB ( .sap ,
see
.BR create ).
Takes effect for this run immediately (declared before the store opens).
.TP
.BI \-\-retain " DURATION"
Continuously drop data older than
.I DURATION
(e.g.
.BR 30d ,
.BR 12h ,
.BR 90m ).
.TP
.BI \-\-retain\-size " SIZE"
Keep the on-disk (compressed) size of the log at or under
.I SIZE
(e.g.
.BR 200G ,
.BR 512M ;
powers of 1024), dropping the oldest data first. Combines with
.BR \-\-retain :
whichever limit bites first wins.
.PP
Retention is checked every second, at startup and once more at shutdown.
Dropping the head currently compacts the log by rewriting the remaining
data, so enforcement is batched: age-expired data is dropped once it makes
up about a tenth of the file, and a size overrun is trimmed down to 95% of
the budget. Compaction briefly needs free disk space proportional to the
retained data.
.SS import
.B timberfs import \-\-into
.I DEST
.IR SOURCE .\|.\|.
converts existing plain log files into a timberfs log. The destination is
a named flag on purpose: a shell glob among the sources can never
swallow it. With
.B \-\-records
the single source (a file, or standard input when none is given) is a
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream: entries arrive pre-framed, an entry carrying its original
write window
.RB ( wf / wl )
keeps it \(em replicating a store preserves its write history \(em
and one without falls back to import's own clock, the entry's parsed
timestamp (the backfill doctrine). Delivery is ATOMIC: nothing is
visible until the stream's
.B stream\-end
arrives, and a truncated stream leaves the store byte-for-byte
unchanged. The upstream selection echo and every pipeline
.B stage=
land in the destination's manifest \(em an artifact remembers the
pipe that filled it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timber\-filter \-\-records \-\-has ERROR \-\-from 13:00 app.log |
    timberfs import \-\-records \-\-into case.timber.d/case.log
timberfs query \-\-records app.log | ssh host timberfs import \-\-records \-\-into mirror/app.log
.RE
.fi Several sources \(em a rotated set \(em may be given
in any order: each file's first parsed timestamp determines the stitch
order (rotation numbering and glob order lie), and the plan is printed
before anything is written. Two sources starting at the same timestamp
are refused as likely duplicates.
.PP
Each plain source is placed by its
.B first timestamp
relative to what the destination already holds. Starting
.I after
the destination's last write, it simply appends (the daily bulk-load).
Starting
.I inside
the destination's window \(em day files cut with slack, an import re-run
\(em the overlap is deduplicated
.BR "line by line" :
lines the destination already has are skipped, genuinely new lines in
the covered window are imported (with a warning, since that usually
means the captures disagreed), and re-importing an already-covered file
is a clean no-op. Counts are reported. Starting
.I before
everything in the destination is refused \(em import in chronological
order or to a new target. A source starting exactly
.B where the destination starts
is treated as the same file, regrown: the already-imported prefix is
byte-verified and only the growth is appended (truncated or rewritten
files are refused before anything is written; see
.BR \-\-quick ).
.PP
A source that is itself a timberfs log \(em a rotation segment shipped
from another machine, say \(em is detected automatically (a
.BR .trunk / .rings
path, or a logical name whose pair exists) and its chunks are merged
.B verbatim
into the destination: no decompression, no timestamp parsing, index
included, cost proportional to the compressed size. A segment whose exact
chunks are already in the destination is skipped, so re-running a
shipping script is a no-op; merging refuses to break the destination's
time ordering (import segments oldest-first). Plain and timberfs sources
can be stitched together. Chunk time
windows come from timestamps
.B parsed from the log lines
(historical data's write time is meaningless), so
.B query
works by the times the log actually talks about. Auto-detected timestamps
must sit at the start of the line: RFC 3339 / ISO 8601 and common
variants (space instead of
.BR T ,
dotted dates, and
.BR . / , / :
before the milliseconds \(em logback's
.B yyyy.MM.dd HH:mm:ss:SSS
included) and leading epoch seconds/milliseconds; Apache/CLF
.RB ( [10/Jul/2026:09:23:45\ +0200] )
is the one mid-line exception. Anything else via
.B \-\-timestamp\-regex
(one capture group, searched \(em anchor with
.B ^
if wanted) plus
.B \-\-timestamp\-format
(chrono strftime). Zoneless timestamps are taken as local time unless
.BR \-\-utc .
Lines without a timestamp (stack traces, continuations) inherit the
previous line's, so multiline entries land in the right window; a file
that STARTS mid-entry backfills its head lines with the first timestamp
found, and in a stitch they inherit the previous file's last stamp \(em
exactly right for entries split by rotation. Mildly out-of-order lines
widen chunk windows instead of getting lost. Accepts
.B \-\-chunk\-size
and
.BR \-\-level ,
and
.B \-\-index
declares and builds the
.B .grain
token index \(em needed once per log (the declaration persists in the
.B .bark
manifest; see
.BR create ),
after which every writer maintains the index automatically.
.B \-\-wal
declares the write-ahead sidecar for future writers of this log \(em a
batch import has no live unflushed tail of its own to protect, so this
only sets the property for the next
.B append
or
.B mount .
.PP
Re-importing is idempotent: a non-empty destination is treated as a
checkpoint. The already-imported bytes are verified against the same
source ranges (every chunk, or first/middle/last with
.BR \-\-quick ),
then only what the source has grown by is appended; an identical source
is a no-op, and a source that shrank or changed is refused before
anything is written (rotated files should be imported to a new target).
.PP
.B \-\-follow
.RB ( \-F )
does not stop at the source's end: it keeps reading as the producer writes,
which is how a log gets into a store when its producer cannot be pointed at
a FIFO \(em or must not be, because nothing in the producer's write path
should depend on this side. Position and rotation are the whole difficulty
and are handled by three rules:
.TP
The store is the checkpoint.
A start re-syncs against the lines the store already holds over the window
the source covers, so a restart can neither lose nor duplicate, and there is
no position file to go stale, be restored out of step, or disagree with the
store. The cost is a scan of the source at startup, which is one reason to
rotate a busy log hourly rather than daily.
.TP
A descriptor is never abandoned before EOF.
When the path stops being the file it was, the file already open is read to
its end BEFORE the new one is opened \(em so the lines written between the
last read and the rename cannot be stranded, which is exactly what a
.B tail \-F
pipeline drops. Rotation therefore needs no pattern while the follower runs;
.BI \-\-rotated " PATH"
exists only for data written while it was NOT running, and defaults to
.IB SOURCE .1
and
.IB SOURCE .0
when they exist. A source that shrank is reported as a truncation
(copytruncate) and re-read from the start.
.TP
Every position decision is announced.
Which file, how much, and why. A follower that silently reads the wrong
thing would be worse than one that stops.
.PP
Entries are stamped from their own timestamps, as in any import, which keeps
a followed store indistinguishable from an imported one \(em where
.B tail \-F | timberfs append
stamps ARRIVAL and so builds a store the two can never reconcile. A
follower is a live writer like the appender: it takes the log's writer lock
for its lifetime, honours declared retention, keeps a declared
.B .grain
current, maintains the
.B .sap
when one is declared, and takes
.BR \-\-flush\-age ,
.B \-\-exit\-on\-upgrade
and
.B \-\-wait\-for\-writer
for that reason.
.BI \-\-poll " SECS"
sets how often it looks (default 1); below
.B \-\-flush\-age
nothing observable improves, since a flushed chunk is the unit of
visibility.
.B \-\-flush\-age
itself defaults to 60 here rather than the appender's 5, because it means
something different: the appender's input is a pipe, so unflushed data
exists only in memory and the age bounds what a crash loses, while a
follower's input is a file that stays on disk and the store is the
checkpoint \(em a partial chunk lost to a crash is simply re-read. The age
therefore only decides how soon new lines become queryable, and setting it
low costs compression instead, since a chunk then holds too little for zstd
to work with (3.1x at five seconds against 7.7x at sixty, at one line a
second). A chunk closes on whichever comes first, that age or
.B \-\-chunk\-size
(256 KiB of uncompressed data), plus once at exit. Waits for a source that does not exist yet rather than failing,
so a unit may start before its producer.
.PP
.B Identity crosses the hop.
Importing FROM a timberfs source (a pair or a bundle) seeds the
destination's manifest from it: the destination mints its own
.IR id ,
records the source as
.IR derived_from ,
and inherits its LABELS \(em while retention, the index and the wal stay
the destination's own, since settings are not provenance. Lineage is
claimed only when exactly one source declares a manifest: a stitched set
of segments from several stores has no single parent, so the import says so
and proceeds without one. A re-import never rewrites a manifest that
already exists.
.SS export
.B timberfs export
.I SOURCE
.B \-\-into
.I DEST
copies the chunks overlapping the given window (default: everything) into
a NEW timberfs log \(em verbatim, no recompression, cost proportional to
the compressed size of the window. The source needs no lock and may be
live. A
.I DEST
ending in
.B .timber
writes the single-file transfer bundle instead: a plain uncompressed tar
(the payload is already zstd) holding the
.B .rings
member first and the
.B .trunk
member second.
Bundles are first-class READ-ONLY logs:
.BR query ,
.B index
and
.B export
operate on a
.B .timber
file directly (tar stores its members contiguously and uncompressed, so
the trunk member is simply a trunk at an offset), and
.B import
merges them \(em unpacking is only ever needed to append. Stock tools always
suffice to recover one:
.B tar xf x.timber && zstd \-dc x.trunk
(a pair tarred by hand follows the same convention). Export always
creates; merging into an existing log is
.BR import 's
job. A bundle never carries a
.B .sap
sidecar even when the source has
.B wal
declared \(em it is live writer state, not archive content.
.PP
A window that selects nothing still exports: an empty artifact whose bark
records the requested window is evidence of absence (\(lqSaturday was
covered, nothing was there \(em ingest Sunday\(rq), where a MISSING file
is absence of evidence (\(lqa day is missing \(em don't ingest past the
gap\(rq). Opposite signals to a consumer, so an empty result is a result,
not an error;
.B \-\-fail\-on\-empty
makes it one for pipelines that want a quiet day to be loud.
.B import
treats empty sources the same way: skipped with a note, never fatal, and
an all-empty import into a new destination still creates it.
.SS query
.B timberfs query
.I FILE
prints the log entries inside the given time window to standard output,
reading the backing files directly \(em it works with or without an active
mount, but only sees flushed chunks (the still-buffered tail, at most
flush-age old, is visible only through the mount).
.I FILE
is a backing file: the logical name, or its
.B .trunk
or
.B .rings
path.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " TIME"
Start of the window (default: beginning of the file).
.TP
.BI \-\-to " TIME"
End of the window (default: end of the file).
.TP
.BI \-\-has " TOKEN"
Only chunks that (probably) contain
.IR TOKEN ,
tested against the
.B .grain
Bloom index (build it with
.BR "timberfs reindex" ;
without one, \-\-has warns and scans the window). Matching is by WHOLE
token \(em ASCII-alphanumeric runs of 3\(en64 characters, exact case;
substrings do not match. An argument containing separators
.RB ( req\-8f3a )
must match all its tokens in the same chunk; repeat
.B \-\-has
for chunk-level AND;
.BI \-\-any " TEXT"
(repeatable) keeps chunks where at least ONE alternative matches \(em
the union of exact branches, still exact \(em and composes with
.BR \-\-has .
About 1% of non-matching chunks pass anyway (Bloom
false positives) \(em the exact, entry-level filtering belongs in
.BR timber\-filter (1)
downstream. The killer use: finding a unique identifier with NO time
bound.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
After the selected output, keep emitting entries as chunks are committed,
until interrupted \(em like
.BR tail (1)
.BR \-f .
When the store declares a
.B wal
.RB ( "timberfs set " \fIstore\fB " wal=true" ),
new entries appear as the writer appends them \(em the follower tails the
.B .sap
sidecar's live edge, so visibility no longer waits for a chunk and
.B \-\-flush\-age
is free to stay long for compression. Without one, a flushed chunk is the
unit of visibility and new data appears within the writer's
.B \-\-flush\-age
instead (5s for the appender, 60s for
.BR "import \-\-follow" ).
Read-only and lock-free either way, so it runs beside a live
appender; plain text streams raw bytes, while
.BR \-0 ", " \-\-records ", and " \-\-show\-write\-time
run the entry pipeline. Filter a live follow with a pipe
.RB ( "| grep" );
.B \-\-has
selects whole chunks offline and does not compose with it.
.IP
In the entry pipeline an entry is normally closed by the NEXT stamped
line, which would leave the newest entry of a store that falls quiet
unemitted \(em exactly the entry an incident cares about. After ten
seconds with no new chunk, the pending entry is therefore closed and
emitted: past any writer's
.BR \-\-flush\-age ,
so a producer mid-entry has committed the rest of it already. A
.B \-\-flush\-age
longer than that can split a multiline entry that a chunk boundary
split first.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-chunk " N"
Resume at chunk
.I N
(the numbers
.B timberfs index
prints), the position a consumer's cursor holds. Requires
.BR \-\-follow ,
and conflicts with
.BR \-\-from / \-\-tail .
Exact where a timestamp is not: a number names one chunk, where
.B \-\-from
can match two that share a boundary millisecond, and chunk numbers only
move forward where the write axis is a wall clock an NTP step can push
backwards (see THE TWO CLOCKS). A number older than anything the store
still holds starts at the oldest chunk it has \(em only the caller knows
its position was dropped, so only the caller can report it.
.TP
.BI \-\-poll " SECS"
Seconds between looks for new data
.RB ( \-\-follow
only). Defaults to 0.2 when there is a live
.B .sap
to tail \(em where this interval IS the latency \(em and 1 otherwise, where
the writer's
.B \-\-flush\-age
decides instead and a faster poll would only spend syscalls.
.TP
.BI \-\-tail " N"
Show (about) the last
.I N
log entries \(em a stamped line and its continuation lines are one entry \(em
then, with
.BR \-\-follow ,
keep going. Chunk-granular: rounded out to a whole chunk, so a few extra may
precede the Nth-from-last. (A store with no parseable timestamps counts lines.)
.TP
.BI \-\-max " N"
Stop after at most
.I N
entries (a hard cap, like
.BR head (1)).
Composes with
.B \-\-follow
to bound it; conflicts with
.BR \-\-tail .
.PP
Several
.I FILE
arguments give the fleet view over per-stream logs: chunks are selected
per file (each file's own rings and grain) and interleaved across files
by their time windows, with within-file order preserved; every output
line carries a grep-style
.RB \(lq path: \(rq
prefix
.RB ( \-\-no\-filename
to suppress). Attribution lives in the filename \(em store one log per
host/app and merge at read time.
.PP
Selection is
.B chunk-granular
by design: every chunk whose write-time window overlaps the requested
range is emitted in full, so the result carries at most flush-age worth of
slop at each edge. The intended workflow is to let
.B query
do the coarse seek into a huge file, then trim exactly with
.BR timber\-filter (1)
on the timestamps the log lines carry anyway.
.SS The same search, as a document
A search is a value, and a whole one can be handed over as JSON instead
of assembled from flags \(em which is what a tool, a client library or a
query server needs.
.TP
.BI \-\-query " FILE"
Read the whole search from a JSON document (\c
.B \-
for stdin) rather than from flags. The two are one question asked two
ways, not two dialects of it. A member this build does not know is an
.BR error ,
never a silently wider search: a misspelled
.B form
for
.B from
must not become an unbounded query that looks like it worked.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-json
Print the document these flags describe, and exit \(em what
.B \-\-query
reads back. The quickest way to learn the format, and the only
description of it that cannot drift:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs query app.log \-\-from 13:42 \-\-has ERROR \-\-max 10 \-\-dump\-json
timberfs query ... \-\-dump\-json | timberfs query \-\-query \-
.RE
.fi
.PP
Note what the round trip does to the store: the flags name it by
.BR path ,
and the document names it by
.B identity
(its id), because a path is neither unique nor stable and says nothing
about what the store holds. In the document a store is chosen by a label
.BR predicate ,
and there is no way to name one by path at all.
.PP
The format, its strictness rule, its versioning and its members are
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5),
with a JSON Schema at
.IR /usr/share/doc/timberfs/query\-document.schema.json .
⚠ It is
.B EXPERIMENTAL
and says so in every document: expect it to be broken in place while it
settles, and upgrade a generator together with the timberfs it talks to.
.SS searching
Matching lives in its own tool:
.BR timber\-filter (1),
the entry-aware pipeline filter. A store argument is searched through
the selection layer (it runs
.B timberfs query \-\-records
itself), so the fused and piped spellings are the same search:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timber\-filter \-\-has ERROR \-\-from 13:00 backing/app.log
timberfs query \-\-records \-\-from 13:00 backing/app.log | timber\-filter \-\-has ERROR
.RE
.fi
.PP
The investigation-as-artifact workflow is a pipe into the records sink
\(em the destination's manifest records the selection and every stage
that shaped the data:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timber\-filter \-\-records \-\-has 'tenantId=FOO' \-\-from 13:00 backing/app.log |
    timberfs import \-\-records \-\-into case/case.log
timberfs export case/case.log \-\-into case.timber   # attach to the ticket
.RE
.fi
.SS info
.B timberfs info
.I FILE
shows a store's vital signs on one screen: identity and creation time,
lineage (what it derives from, by which operation, over which requested
window, and \(em for records-sink
artifacts \(em the command line that produced it, labelled
.BR question ),
declared provenance, logical size vs on-disk size and compression
ratio, the time span covered and its duration, index sizes with grain
.B coverage
(a grain lagging its log \(em the newest chunks of a live writer,
partial extends \(em covers fewer chunks than the rings; the gap is
scanned), whether
.B wal
is declared and how many bytes currently sit in its
.B .sap
sidecar unflushed (a read-only stat \(em never replayed by
.BR info ),
and the writer state (none / active appender or import / mounted, probed
from the actual flocks, never from stale lock-file contents). A store
with registered followers also gets a
.B followers
block: each one furthest behind first, with how far behind it is, how
much of the store it alone is holding, how much it has delivered, which
of them are
.B retaining
and whether each is running (see
.BR FOLLOWERS ).
A store still declaring the superseded
.B cursors
key gets its
.B consumers
block too, under a line saying so.
Works identically on backing pairs and read-only
.B .timber
bundles \(em a bundle is a snapshot, so nothing holds a position in one.
.B \-\-json
prints the same facts as one JSON object for scripting. Read-only and
safe against live writers.
.SS identity
.B timberfs identity
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-mint ]
.RB [ \-\-keep
.IR index | manifest ]
reports, and on request repairs, the id that makes a backing pair a
store. The id is a fact rather than a setting, so
.B set
refuses it; this is the verb for the three ways it can be broken.
.PP
With no flag it only reports \(em what the manifest says, what the
.B .rings
header says, and a verdict \(em and exits non\-zero when the two do not
agree or there is no identity at all, so it doubles as the check a
script runs.
.TP
.B \-\-mint
make an identity for a pair that carries none on either side. Refused
where one already exists: that is a
.B \-\-keep
question.
.B timberfs create \-\-if\-not\-exists
does the same thing as part of provisioning.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " index"
resolve a disagreement by keeping what the backing pair says and writing
it into the manifest. The usual answer, because the pair
.I is
the store: reach for it after a manifest was hand\-edited, restored from
an older backup, or copied in from elsewhere.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " manifest"
the other side of the same repair, for a pair restored under a manifest
you trust.
.PP
Repair rewrites the
.B .rings
header, which a live writer also rewrites when retention drops a head,
so it refuses to run while one holds the store \(em stop the writer
first. Log data is never touched by any of this.
.PP
Choosing wrongly is not cosmetic: a follower records its store by
identity, so the side you discard is the one whose cursors stop
resolving.
.SS index
.B timberfs index
.I FILE
prints the chunk index of a backing file: per chunk the uncompressed
offset and length, compressed length, compression ratio, and first/last
write time, plus totals.
.SS list
.B timberfs list
.RI [ DIR " ...]"
is the directory-level complement to
.BR info :
one row per store, sorted by forest then handle, across every configured
.B forest
(see
.B FORESTS
below), or \(em when one or more
.I DIR
arguments are given \(em exactly the stores in those directories
(ad-hoc; they need not be configured forests). Columns:
.B ID
(the first 8 characters of the store's id \(em a prefix
.B info
accepts;
.B \-\-full\-id
prints all of it),
.B NAME
(the name the store DECLARES, falling back to its forest handle when it
declares none \(em which is what lets a store have an opaque path and a
readable name),
.B FOREST
(the config filename minus
.IR .conf ,
or the directory itself for an ad-hoc/
.B TIMBERFS_FORESTS
forest),
.B SIZE
(compressed on-disk size),
.B SPAN
(earliest \(em latest write time covered, or
.IR empty ),
.B WRITER
.RI ( live
if a writer currently holds the file's own lock, else
.IR \- ),
.B INDEX
.RI ( grain
if a
.B .grain
token index is present or declared, else
.IR \- ),
and
.B RETAIN
(declared
.BR retain / retain_size ,
else
.IR \- ),
and
.B LABELS
(what the store declares about itself, truncated to fit).
A
.B FOLLOWERS
column is added when at least one listed store has something to say
there \(em how many readers hold a position in it and the state of the
worst of them
.RI ( "2, 6d 2h behind" ", " "1, caught up" ", " "2, GAP" ", " "1, never run" );
.I \-
means nothing is registered and nothing declared, which is not the same
as
.IR 0 ,
something declared with nothing reading it (see
.BR FOLLOWERS ).
The same handle found in two forests shows up as two rows \(em
.B list
is how an ambiguity that handle resolution would refuse to guess at
becomes visible, not something it hides.
.TP
.BI \-\-select " EXPR"
List only the stores whose manifest satisfies
.IR EXPR ,
the same predicate the query document takes. Comma-separated terms are
ANDed;
.BR key=value ", " key!=value ", " key=~regex " and " key!~regex
(regexes anchored at both ends). An absent label reads as the empty
string, so
.B key=
selects the stores that do NOT declare it and
.B key!=
the ones that do.
.B *
is every store, as an omitted
.B \-\-select
is. Quote a value that must contain a comma.
.RS
.PP
It matches the WHOLE manifest, not labels alone \(em a name, an id and a
setting are as matchable as a label. Nothing is held back for being the
wrong KIND of fact: a label is unique and stable where a name is neither,
but that is a difference in what a match GUARANTEES, not in what you are
allowed to ask.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-forest " NAME"
Only this forest, by the name
.B timberfs forest list
gives it; repeatable. Without it every configured forest is listed.
Conflicts with a
.I DIR
argument, which is the ad-hoc form of the same thing.
.TP
.B \-\-full\-id
Print each store's whole id instead of the leading 8 characters. The
short form is a prefix
.B info
accepts, so this is for copying an id somewhere that wants all of it.
.TP
.B \-\-names
Bare handles only, one per line, no header or columns \(em what shell
completion consumes.
.TP
.B \-\-json
A JSON array of objects
.RI ( handle ", " forest ", " dir ", " path ", " size_bytes ", "
.IR from_ms ", " to_ms ", " writer_live ", " indexed ", " retain ", "
.IR retain_size ", " id ", " created ", " labels ", " first_seq ", "
.IR last_seq ", " next_seq ", " dropped_chunks ", " dropped_bytes ", "
.IR dropped_uncompressed_bytes ", " followers ", "
.IR cursors_dir ", " consumers ", " held_bytes )
instead of the human table.
.I id
is the store's durable identity, or
.B null
for a store with no manifest \(em what anything holding a position against
it records, and never a path.
.I labels
is the manifest's provenance, verbatim (see
.BR PROVENANCE ).
The
.I \%*_seq
and
.I dropped_*
fields carry the same names and meanings as
.BR info \(aqs ,
deliberately: a consumer reading both must not have to remember which shape
each chose. See
.B WHAT A STORE HAS DROPPED
for what they mean and when to distrust them.
.I followers
is always an array \(em the registry knows every follower of every
store, so empty means empty.
.I cursors_dir
and
.I consumers
carry the superseded key, and are
.B null
when the store declares no directory, while
.I consumers
is an empty array when it declares one that holds no position in this
store \(em the same distinction the table's
.I \-
and
.I 0
make.
.PP
No forests configured and no
.I DIR
given prints a note to stderr and exits 0; a forest or
.I DIR
that doesn't exist is skipped (also noted). Read-only and lock-free \(em
like
.BR info ,
it only ever probes a writer lock, never acquires one, so it works for a
non-root reader on a root-owned forest.
.SS reindex
.B timberfs reindex
.I FILE
builds (or rebuilds) the
.B .grain
token index for a log: one Bloom filter per chunk over every distinct
token in it, ~10 bits per token, ~1% false positives \(em roughly 1\(en2%
of the raw data size. Derived data under the sidecar contract: safe to
delete and cheap to rebuild, so nothing here can lose data. A retention
head-drop does NOT drop it \(em the index is trimmed by the same chunk
prefix the store was, without decompressing anything \(em but any other
rewrite of the chunk numbering does, and this is how you get it back. A
grain that lags a growing log is still correct \(em uncovered chunks are
simply scanned.
.SS trim
.B timberfs trim
.I STORE
enforces the store's DECLARED retention once, now.
.PP
Load-bearing rather than convenient. Retention runs inside a live
WRITER \(em every appender, mount and intake enforces it on its own tick \(em
so a store whose producer went quiet keeps its data indefinitely, and
under
.B retain_unconsumed
that means keeping data already shipped off the box. This is the cron-able
answer to that, and deliberately NOT the tempting shortcut of letting a
follower collapse the head itself, which would make a reader a writer and
put two of them on one head.
.PP
A store somebody else is writing is left ALONE and said so: that writer's
own tick is already doing this, once a second, and taking its lock away to
repeat the work is the one thing this must not do. A store that declares
no retention is a no-op, not an error \(em which is what a cron entry over a
whole forest needs.
.TP
.B \\-\\-dry\\-run
Report how many chunks INTEREST would drop, and change nothing. Age and
size are deliberately not previewed: both carry hysteresis and are decided
by the writer at the moment it acts, so a number here would be a guess.
.PP
Reports the chunk NUMBERS it took, not just a count \(em after a head-drop
the survivors keep their numbers, so a count says nothing about which
chunks went, and those numbers are what a follower's position is compared
against.
.SS rotate
.B timberfs rotate \-\-cutoff
.I TIME SOURCE
.RI [ DEST ]
moves every chunk written entirely before
.I TIME
out of
.I SOURCE
and appends it to
.I DEST
(created if missing, must be in the same backing directory). Compressed
frames are relocated verbatim \(em nothing is recompressed \(em so the
cost is proportional to the compressed size. A chunk straddling the cutoff
stays in the source. Appending to an existing
.I DEST
is refused if it would break the destination's time ordering.
.PP
Rotation auto-detects a live mount: if a daemon serves the backing
directory (advertised via the lock file), the request is routed through it
and performed atomically; otherwise the backing files are rewritten
directly under the same lock.
.PP
Rotating nothing into a new
.I DEST
still creates it, empty \(em the same present-but-empty vs missing
distinction as an empty
.BR export ,
for pipelines that rotate-then-ship.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Drop the rotated chunks instead of moving them (retention). Mutually
exclusive with
.IR DEST .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would move without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-on\-empty
Error when nothing rotates instead of attesting the empty result.
.SS forest
Declare where stores live. A
.I forest
is a directory
.B timberfs
searches, and it is the ONE thing a
.B timberfs
command names by
.B path
\(em every other argument names a
.IR store ,
and a store is found by what it declares. See
.B FORESTS
below.
.PP
Declaring a forest here means
.I /etc/timberfs/forests.d
stops being the interface: the file is still what gets read, and reading
or hand-editing it keeps working, but nothing has to be hand-edited to
get a forest.
.TP
.B timberfs forest create DIR [\-\-name N] [\-\-dry\-run]
Create
.I DIR
if it is not there, and declare it. A relative
.I DIR
is made absolute against the current directory, because the daemons that
read this config have a different one.
.I \-\-name
defaults to
.IR DIR 's
last component and is constrained to
.B [A\-Za\-z0\-9_.\-]
\(em it is the config file's name, and it appears in every ambiguity
message.
.RS
.PP
Idempotent: re-declaring the same directory under the same name is a
no-op, so provisioning may run it on every boot.
.PP
Three things are refused rather than written, all of them the same
problem \(em a store with two forests to belong to:
.B the same directory under a second name;
.B a directory that is inside an existing forest;
and
.B a directory that contains one.
A forest is scanned at its root AND one directory deep, so any of those
makes the stores between them members of both, and a handle that matches
two stores cannot be resolved. Re-pointing an existing name is refused
too:
.B remove
it first, deliberately, since every store under it would be stranded.
.RE
.TP
.B timberfs forest list [\-\-names] [\-\-json]
What is declared, and whether it is
.B usable:
the store count, and whether the directory is
.B MISSING
or
.BR READONLY .
That last part is the point \(em an undeclared or unwritable forest
otherwise surfaces as "store not found" somewhere far away, at write
time, in another process.
.TP
.B timberfs forest remove NAME [\-\-dry\-run]
Un-declare a forest. The directory and every store in it are left
exactly as they are; the path and the store count are printed so an
operator who did mean to delete the data knows where it is. This removes
a declaration, never data.
.SS follower
Manage a store's
.IR followers :
registered readers, each with a name, a type, a
.B retaining
flag and a durable position. See
.B FOLLOWERS
below for what a follower IS and why it is a declared object rather than
a cursor found lying in a directory.
.TP
.B timberfs follower create NAME \-\-store STORE [options]
Register a follower.
.I NAME
is host-unique and is also the systemd instance
.RB ( timberfs\-follower@ NAME ),
which is why it is constrained to
.BR [A\-Za\-z0\-9_.\-] :
no
.BR systemd\-escape (1),
so the name in
.B systemctl status
is the name that was typed. Refused if taken \(em a collision is a
registration error, not two processes overwriting one position.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-store " STORE"
The store to follow: a path, or a forest handle. Recorded by IDENTITY
(its
.B .bark
.IR id ,
minted here when the store has none), never by path \(em a store can
move, and a path can come to hold a different store.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
What runs it.
.B otlp
(the default) execs
.BR timber\-otlp (1),
shipping ENTRIES over OTLP to anything that speaks it.
.B frames
execs
.B timberfs frames\-send
instead, shipping CHUNKS verbatim to a
.B frames\-intake
\(em see REPLICATION. A frames follower takes no
.BR \-\-start :
it resumes from the RECEIVER's position, which is authoritative, so there
is no local decision about where to begin and no way to re-ship a store by
getting one wrong.
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " URL"
Where it ships to. Promoted out of
.I ARGS
because every follower type has a destination and it is the first thing
an operator reads in
.BR list .
.TP
.B \-\-retaining
Declare that this follower's position holds the store's head back:
retention keeps what it has not read ON TOP OF what age and size keep,
never as a cap on them \(em
.B retain_size
still overrides it, and the writer records what was dropped unread (see
.BR RETENTION ).
Takes effect only where the store declares
.BR retain_unconsumed .
.B A retaining follower with no position holds EVERYTHING
\(em which is the point (it is what protects a follower deployed before
it first runs) and equally the footgun, the same one an unused Postgres
replication slot has. Start it.
.TP
.BR \-\-enable ", " \-\-start
.BR systemctl (1)
enable and/or start the unit. systemd's two verbs, kept distinct.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the declaration and the command line it would run; register
nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\- " ARGS" .\|.\|.
Everything after
.B \-\-
is passed to the shipper verbatim, in the shipper's own spelling:
.B \-\- \-\-service checkout \-\-compress gzip
Deliberately NOT re-declared here \(em a second spelling of the same
flags is a second thing to keep in step, and it would drift.
.PP
Two flags are DERIVED, and an explicit one in
.I ARGS
always wins over the derivation.
.B \-\-cursor
is the follower's own position file in the registry.
.B \-\-start
follows
.BR retaining :
.B begin
for a retaining follower, since
.B end
would skip on the first run exactly the backlog it was registered to
protect and let retention then drop it;
.B end
otherwise.
.RE
.TP
.B timberfs follower list [\-\-store STORE] [\-\-names] [\-\-json]
Every registered follower, by name: its store, type, whether it retains,
its position, how far behind it is and whether it is
.BR running .
.B \-\-store
narrows to one store's followers.
.B \-\-names
prints bare names, one per line \(em what shell completion consumes.
.TP
.B timberfs follower status NAME [\-\-json]
One follower on one screen: what it declares, where its position stands
in the store, whether anything is honouring its
.B retaining
flag, and whether it is running. That last line is read from the
follower's
.BR lock ,
not from systemd: a lock is released by the kernel on process death, so
it cannot go stale, while a unit's state answers about the unit.
.TP
.B timberfs follower update NAME [KEY=VALUE]... [\-\-unset KEY] [\-\- ARGS...]
Change a declaration.
.I KEY
is
.BR retaining ,
.BR endpoint ,
.B type
or
.BR store ;
a
.B \-\-
tail replaces the shipper's arguments wholesale. Validated before it is
written, so an update that would make a follower unrunnable fails now
rather than at its next start. A running follower keeps the OLD
declaration until it is restarted, and is told so.
.IP
.B update NAME retaining=false
is the first half of retiring a follower. It quantifies what it frees,
and says the part that is easy to miss: the FLAG toggles, its EFFECT
does not \(em setting it back to
.B true
will not bring dropped data back, and the follower then resumes at a
position that may be gapped.
.TP
.B timberfs follower delete NAME [\-\-stop] [\-\-disable]
Unregister a follower: bookkeeping, once the head has been released.
Refused while it is
.B retaining
(release it deliberately first, above) and while it is RUNNING \(em
deleting it under a live process would leave that process writing an
unlinked position file, silently doing nothing. Both refusals are about
deliberateness rather than prevention, so there is no
.BR \-\-force :
the two-step IS the force. One escape: a follower whose store no longer
exists deletes freely, there being nothing to release.
.TP
.B timberfs follower run NAME
Read the declaration and EXEC its shipper \(em what
.B timberfs\-follower@.service
runs. A dispatcher, not a supervisor: the process is REPLACED, so
systemd keeps the lifecycle, the restarts and the journal, and timberfs
runs no daemon of its own. The follower's lock is taken here and
inherited across the exec, so a shipper needs no lock code of its own
and a second
.B run
of the same follower is refused.
.SS forward\-intake
.B timberfs forward\-intake \-\-forest
.I NAME
receives the Fluentd Forward protocol v1 over TCP \(em the wire protocol of
Docker's
.B fluentd
log driver, Fluent Bit, Fluentd, and the fluent\-logger client libraries \(em
and writes each tag into its own store under
.IR DIR ,
in a directory of its own:
.IB DIR/TAG / TAG .log\fR.
That is the layout every timberfs intake writes, so a store is named after
the tag and nothing about the protocol that delivered it \(em
.B timberfs query
.I TAG
resolves it by that name.
One connection multiplexes many tags; a container's stdout and stderr share
one tag and interleave in arrival order (the
.B source
field is dropped).
.PP
Supports the Message, Forward and PackedForward transport modes (str and
bin blobs both), event times as unix seconds or the EventTime extension
type (sub\-second precision), and Docker's split\-line
.B partial_message
reassembly. A sender that attaches a
.B chunk
id to a batch is acked (a
.B {"ack": id}
reply on the same connection) only once that batch is durable in the
store's write\-ahead sidecar \(em every store this receiver touches
declares
.B "wal": true
\(em so acks flow at fsync rate while chunk compression keeps its own
size/age cadence: the at\-least\-once guarantee a pipe into
.B append
could never express, without shredding the store into per\-line chunks.
.B CompressedPackedForward
(gzip) is refused: the connection is logged and closed rather than
silently dropping data.
.PP
Deliberate limitations, all downstream of Forward v1 having no
authentication or negotiation phase: no TLS (deploy on loopback or a
private network only), no HELO/PING/PONG handshake (clients transition
straight to event transport, which is spec\-legal and how Docker's driver
already works), and no UDP heartbeat listener. Event times are whole
seconds unless the sender opts into sub\-second precision.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Address to listen on (default
.BR 127.0.0.1:24224 ).
Ignored under systemd socket activation (LISTEN_PID/LISTEN_FDS set), which
takes the already\-bound listener instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-forest " NAME"
The forest to write into, as
.B timberfs forest list
names it: one store per tag, each in its own directory,
.IB DIR/TAG / TAG .log\fR.
See
.B WHERE AN INTAKE WRITES
below.
Stores are pre\-created by the operator by default (see
.BR \-\-auto\-create ),
in which case nothing is created here and no write permission on
.I DIR
is needed.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-create
Create a store for a never\-seen tag automatically \(em the Docker\-host
mode, where tags are container names that come and go. By default an
unknown tag is REFUSED: creation is the operator's decision
.RB ( "timberfs create \-\-wal" ),
not the network's. A refused tag is logged once, its events are dropped
and its
.B chunk
ids are never acked \(em an acking sender buffers and retries until the
store exists, so provisioning converges with nothing lost.
.TP
.BI \-\-payload\-key " KEY"
Record field holding the log line (default
.BR log ,
Docker's convention). Falls back to the whole record as compact JSON when
the key is missing or not a string.
.TP
.BI \-\-retain " DURATION"
Declared on every store this receiver creates; same meaning as
.BR append 's
.BR \-\-retain .
.TP
.BI \-\-retain\-size " SIZE"
Declared on every store this receiver creates; same meaning as
.BR append 's
.BR \-\-retain\-size .
.TP
.B \-\-index
Declare and maintain the
.B .grain
token index on every store this receiver creates.
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-on\-upgrade
Exit for a clean re\-exec when this binary is upgraded on disk; the systemd
unit sets it.
.PP
The verb name is provisional.
.SS incus\-intake
.B timberfs incus\-intake
.BI \-\-forest " NAME"
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-project
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR LABELS ]
.RB [ \-\-prefix
.IR TEMPLATE ]
.RB [ \-\-only
.IR NAME ]...
.RB [ \-\-include\-vms ]
.RB [ \-\-index ]
.RB [ \-\-retain
.IR DUR ]
taps the consoles of incus instances into timberfs over the local incus
unix socket, following incus's lifecycle events so an instance started
later is tapped too.
.PP
The console is the
.I everything else
channel: boot output, a crashing JVM's fatal log, whatever a process
writes on its way down. An application that ships its own logs is better
served by
.BR otlp\-intake ;
this is for the output that arrives when the application's own logging is
already dead. Incus keeps only the last 128 KiB of it, in a ring buffer
that wraps in silence, so what is not collected is not recoverable.
.PP
The live console websocket is the feed, not that ring. Reading the ring is
a
.B destructive drain
\(em what it returns is gone from it, including for a human running
.B incus console \-\-show\-log
\(em and everything drained in one poll would carry that poll's timestamp,
collapsing write\-time granularity to the poll interval. So the tap
attaches FIRST and then drains, which both recovers the backlog the
instance produced before the tap arrived and makes that backlog overlap
the live feed rather than leaving a gap between them. Draining continues
for as long as the tap is attached \(em see
.B \-\-drain\-every
below for why, and what it costs.
.PP
⚠ An instance's console is
.BR exclusive .
While this holds one,
.B incus console
on that instance is refused unless forced, and forcing it takes the
console away from the tap (which reconnects).
.TP
.BI \-\-key " LABELS"
which facts identify a store, comma\-separated. Default
.BR type,incus.project,incus.instance :
one store per instance. Add
.B image
for one store per image version; give only
.B type
to put every console on the host into one store. Whatever is named here
is written as a label, so a store can always be found again by the key
that made it \(em which is also why an episodic fact in the key means a
new store each time it changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " TEMPLATE"
what to put in front of a line that STARTS an entry, over the same facts
plus
.BR {time} .
Default
.BR "{time} " .
A store several instances share wants
.B "{time} {incus.instance} "
so its lines say which one wrote them. A
.B {time}
that is not first is fine: the store is given a
.B timestamp_regex
that finds it, since timberfs's own detection is anchored. A template with
no
.B {time}
at all is legal and warned about \(em those entries carry no timestamp of
their own.
.PP
Only lines that start an entry are prefixed. A line carrying its own
timestamp is left alone, and so is a line that arrives while an entry is
open \(em an exception body, a stack frame. An entry that has gone quiet
for
.B \-\-idle
milliseconds (default 100) is closed, which is how a stack trace stays one
entry while the next message does not get swallowed into it.
.PP
Stores are found by their labels, never by a name assembled from them, and
live in directories named after their own id: the readable name is in the
manifest, where
.B timberfs list
shows it. A key matching several existing stores is refused \(em there is
then no one store to write to.
.PP
Each attach writes a marker line naming the instance, its image, its
entrypoint and its uuid: facts that change when a container is rebuilt, so
they belong in the timeline rather than in labels that would claim today's
values for all of history. It also records how much ring backlog followed
it, which marks the seam.
.PP
.PP
⚠ The ring is CONSUMED as the tap streams, not left to accumulate. The two
feeds are independent, so while a tap is attached the ring quietly fills
with its own copy of what has already been written \(em and the next
attach would replay it, duplicating up to 128 KiB on every restart. Each
websocket read is the notification that the ring has grown, so this needs
no timer and a silent instance costs nothing; the drain is rate\-limited
by
.B \-\-drain\-every
(default 30s), which is also the bound on how much an UNCLEAN kill can
duplicate. The cost is that
.B incus console \-\-show\-log
has nothing to show for a tapped instance, which is the point: timberfs
now holds that content, indexed and retained.
.B \-\-keep\-ring
opts out and accepts the duplication.
.PP
Each attach writes a
.B marker
line carrying the instance's image, entrypoint and uuid \(em facts that
change when a container is rebuilt, so they belong in the timeline rather
than in labels, which would claim today's values for all of history.
.B \-\-no\-marker
suppresses it.
.PP
Containers only, unless
.B \-\-include\-vms
\(em a VM's console is file\-backed (so reading it is not a drain) and
carries the kernel's boot output rather than an application's stdout.
Reaching the socket needs membership of the
.B incus\-admin
group.
.SS otlp\-intake
Receive OTLP/HTTP logs \(em the OpenTelemetry protocol every SDK and the
Collector speak \(em and write each stream into its own store in the
forest named by
.IR \-\-forest .
The mirror of
.BR timber\-otlp (1),
which ships the same wire format out.
.PP
It is a subcommand rather than a filter for the reason
.B forward\-intake
is: the HTTP response IS the acknowledgement, and only the writer can
promise durability. A
.B 200
is returned once the batch is fsynced into the store's
.B .sap
write-ahead sidecar (every store this receiver touches declares
.BR wal ),
so a sender's retry logic and the store's chunk cadence stay independent.
.PP
OTLP's structure lands on timberfs's: one
.B ResourceLogs
is one stream, routed by
.BR \-\-route ;
its resource attributes are what the store IS and are seeded into the
.B .bark
manifest when the store is created; each
.B LogRecord
is one entry. A body that already opens with a parseable timestamp is
written verbatim \(em so a store shipped out with
.BR timber\-otlp (1)
and received here comes back byte for byte \(em while an unstamped body is
prefixed with
.RB \(lq "<RFC3339> <SEVERITY> " \(rq
so the store stays time-indexable. Record attributes and any
.BR trace_id / span_id
trail the body as
.IR k = v ,
where
.B query \-\-has
can find them.
.PP
Stores are pre-created by the operator by default; an undeclared stream is
answered
.B 503
with
.BR Retry\-After ,
so the sender buffers and retries until the store exists and provisioning
converges with nothing lost \(em the same contract as an unacked Forward
chunk, spelled in HTTP.
.PP
Both OTLP/HTTP encodings are accepted \(em binary protobuf
.RB ( application/x\-protobuf ,
what every sender defaults to) and JSON \(em and a gzipped body is
inflated, so a stock OpenTelemetry Collector needs nothing configured
beyond the endpoint. The response comes back in the encoding the request
used; a full success in protobuf is the empty message, zero bytes.
.PP
Deliberate limitations, each refused explicitly and by name:
.BR POST\ /v1/logs
only (traces and metrics are not a log store's job \(em 404); no chunked
bodies (411 \(em a receiver that must acknowledge durability needs to know
what it is acknowledging); no TLS (loopback or a private network); and no
gRPC \(em put a Collector in front for :4317.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Address to listen on (default
.BR 127.0.0.1:4318 ,
the OTLP/HTTP port). systemd socket activation on fd 3 is used instead when
.BR LISTEN_PID / LISTEN_FDS
name this process.
.TP
.BI \-\-forest " NAME"
The forest to write into, as
.B timberfs forest list
names it: one store per stream, each in its own directory named after the
route value,
.IB DIR/NAME / NAME .log\fR.
See
.B WHERE AN INTAKE WRITES
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-route " ATTR"
Resource attribute whose value names the store (default
.BR service.name ).
A batch without it is stored as OTel's own
.BR unknown_service ,
never a guess at a better name. The value is a network-supplied name, so it
is sanitized to one path component.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-create
Create a store for a never-seen stream automatically. Default: refuse
undeclared streams, as above.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-body " SIZE"
Largest request body accepted (default
.BR 16M ).
.TP
.BI \-\-retain " DURATION" ", " \-\-retain\-size " SIZE" ", " \-\-index
Declared on every store this receiver creates; same meaning as for
.BR forward\-intake .
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-on\-upgrade
Exit for a clean re\-exec when this binary is upgraded on disk; the systemd
unit sets it.
.PP
The verb name is provisional.
.SS frames\-intake
Receive the native replication wire: compressed chunks land in the
destination's
.I .trunk
VERBATIM and a record in its
.IR .rings ,
so nothing is decompressed at either end and the store is byte-identical
to its source. One store per stream in the forest named by
.BR \-\-forest ,
named by
.B \-\-route
as the other intakes name theirs. Blocking, one thread per connection, no
TLS \(em a private network or a tunnel.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Default
.BR 127.0.0.1:4319 .
.TP
.B \-\-replica
Keep the sender's chunk NUMBERING and record its origin, so
.RI ( origin_id ", " seq )
names the same bytes at both ends. Refused when the numbering would not
continue exactly \(em see REPLICATION. Without it the destination
renumbers and claims no origin, which is weaker and always possible.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-create
Create a store for a never-seen stream. Default: refuse it and say so,
as the other intakes do.
.TP
.BR \-\-index ", " \-\-wal
The receiving store's OWN policy. Settings never travel; only labels do.
.SS frames\-send
Ship a store over the same wire. Sends what the receiver says it LACKS, so
re-running is a no-op rather than a re-send \(em the receiver's position
is authoritative and a sender keeps none of its own.
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " ADDR"
host:port of a
.BR frames\-intake .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Keep shipping as chunks seal, on the same connection. What a registered
.B \-\-type frames
follower runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-cursor " PATH"
Record what the far end has ACKNOWLEDGED, so
.B retain_unconsumed
knows what has left this box. Not a resume point: the receiver's coverage
is what a resume reads.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " DUR"
Socket read/write timeout (default 30s). A replication run that stalls
mid-stream fails and is re-run \(em the receiver's position is
authoritative, so a re-run resumes rather than re-sending.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sidecars
Ship no
.I .grain
pages, leaving the receiver to rebuild its own index. Shipping them is the
default because otherwise a receiver that maintains a token index must
decompress every chunk to re-tokenize what the sender already computed.

.SH WHAT A STORE HAS DROPPED
A store records what has LEFT it over its whole life: how many chunks, and
how many bytes both compressed and not.
.B timberfs info
prints it on the
.B numbering
line and
.B \-\-json
carries it as
.IR dropped_chunks ", " dropped_bytes " and " dropped_uncompressed_bytes .
.PP
.B The count is exact; the size is a FLOOR.
.I dropped_chunks
comes from the numbering, which always knows it: numbering is dense from 0
and only a PREFIX ever drops, so the oldest surviving chunk number IS the
lifetime count. (It is recorded as well, which frees it from assuming the
numbering started at 0.)
.PP
The bytes have to be RECORDED because they cannot be recovered \(em a
head-drop rebases the survivors' offsets, leaving no trace in the index \(em
so
.I dropped_bytes
covers only the drops performed by a binary that records them. A store that
dropped under an older one is missing that earlier loss permanently. The
counters begin accumulating at the store's NEXT drop, so no store has to be
recreated to start collecting, and neither figure is ever
.BR null .
.PP
.B info
says it in words, and says which of the two a zero is \(em a bare
"0 B on disk" beside a store that dropped gigabytes reads as a broken tool.
The distinction is unambiguous because a real chunk carries a frame header
and can never compress to nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
8 dropped (2.2 KiB on disk, 32.1 KiB uncompressed)
8 dropped (size not measured yet)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Note:
.I dropped_bytes
is a lower bound, not a total \(em treat it as "at least this much".
.SH PROVENANCE
A manifest holds two kinds of key, and only one of them is a LABEL.
.B What the store is
\(em identity
.RI ( id ", " created ),
lineage
.RI ( derived_from ", " derived_op ", " window_from ", " window_to ),
operational settings
.RI ( index ", " wal ", " retain ", " retain_size ", " retain_unconsumed ", "
.IR cursors )
and content format
.RI ( timestamp_regex ", " timestamp_format ", " timestamp_utc )
\(em is about the store itself.
.B Where its entries came from
is everything else, and that is provenance: free-form, operator-owned,
and what a fleet view selects on.
.PP
.B The minimum is
.BR host :
the machine that PRODUCED the entries, not the hop that delivered them. No
amount of reading a line recovers it once the entries have been shipped
somewhere else, so whatever writes a store first is the only thing that can
say. What each intake supplies today:
.TP
.BR timberfs\-log@ ", " timberfs\-text@ ", " timberfs\-follow@
.B host=%H
via an
.B ExecStartPre
.BR "timberfs set" ,
plus whatever
.B DECLARE
adds.
.TP
.B otlp\-intake
.IR host ", " service.name ", " service
and every other resource attribute, from the wire \(em an OTLP sender
describes itself, so nothing is guessed.
.TP
.B forward\-intake
.I host
when the sender declares one (the Forward protocol carries none, so the
record is checked for
.IR hostname ", " host ", " nodename " or " source_host ,
and the sender's word is law), always
.I peer
\(em the connecting address, which the receiver knows for certain and which
is not a hostname and is not called one \(em plus
.IR tag ", " container_id " and " container_name .
.TP
.BR append ", " import
nothing, unless
.B \-\-set
says so. A store written by a bare pipe declares nothing, which is a
legitimate state and not an error.
.PP
.B The stream's own name is the store's HANDLE.
There is no separate key for it: a fleet keys on
.I host
plus the handle, so
.B apache\-error
on
.B apache01
and
.B apache02
is two stores differing in one label.
.B timberfs list \-\-json
exposes provenance under
.I labels
and the handle as
.IR handle ,
which is what makes a catalogue a projection of
.B list
rather than a new thing.
.PP
.B Note: a hop can rename a store,
so this holds within one host and needs care across the network. After
shipping, the handle is the DESTINATION's name for the store \(em an OTLP
receiver routes by
.IR service.name ,
so the origin's store name arrives as a label rather than as a handle. A
view spanning hops therefore keys on provenance and never on the handle.
Nothing yet carries the origin's handle verbatim; see
.B ROADMAP
for the identity work that would.
.PP
Values are kept EXACTLY as declared. A dotted key such as
.I service.name
is not flattened: that is a consumer's requirement (Loki's label names
forbid the dot; timberfs does not) and doing it here would lose the key the
operator actually wrote, and silently merge two keys that flatten alike.
.SH RETENTION
Retention is a property of the LOG, declared in its manifest, on THREE
axes:
.B retain
= keep at least this long,
.B retain_size
= a compressed-size budget with the oldest dropped first, and
.B retain_unconsumed
= keep what this store's retaining
.B FOLLOWERS
have not read. They combine with
.BR max ,
never
.BR min :
each names a head prefix it would be happy to see gone, and the largest
wins, so no axis can hold data another one has released. Enforced by
.B every
writer while it holds the writer locks: the appender and the mount
daemon on their once-a-second tick, and
.B import
after each run \(em so timer-mode (cron import) stores and mounted
stores get retention too, with no appender running. Declare it at
.BR create ,
with
.BR set ,
or via the appender's
.BR \-\-retain / \-\-retain\-size
flags (which persist the declaration \(em all roads converge, and a
restarted writer can no longer silently lose its policy). Absent keys
mean no limits: an artifact carved by
.B grep \-\-into
has no business expiring, which is also why retention, like all
settings, is never inherited by derived stores.
.B timberfs info
shows the declared policy and calls out a store that is over budget
with no writer running to enforce it \(em which
.BR "timberfs trim" ,
above, is the one-shot answer to.
.PP
.B The third axis needs the second.
.B retain_unconsumed
is refused without a
.BR retain_size ,
and that is a design constraint rather than a validation nicety: interest
only ever holds MORE, so with no budget beside it one stalled follower
pins the store until the disk fills, which kills the PRODUCER \(em losing
the newest data to protect the oldest, strictly the worse trade. An age
window is not a substitute: it is a bet on how long a link stays down,
which is the very thing this axis exists to stop anyone making.
.PP
.B Note:
the CAP, not the consumption rule, is what decides an outage: it has
to be sized as ingest-rate times the outage worth surviving. Interest
retention does not remove that sizing \(em it removes the STEADY-STATE
hoarding, the weeks of already-shipped bytes kept just in case, which is
the actual win.
.PP
When the budget does override a follower, the loss is recorded EXACTLY,
by the writer, at the moment it happens:
.PP
.RS
.nf
retain_size (50.0 KiB) reached with follower central at chunk 4200
  \(em dropped chunks 4200..4830 it had not read
.fi
.RE
.PP
That is owed rather than optional. With finite disk, bounded loss is a
choice already made \(em the alternative is blocking the producer, which
for telemetry is worse than losing an hour of access logs \(em so what is
owed is precise accounting at the moment it happens, and the writer holds
both halves of the comparison right there.
.BR timber\-otlp (1)\'s
.B GAP
warning is the same fact inferred later, from the other side, bounded only
by timestamps; this one is exact.
.SH FOLLOWERS
A
.I follower
is a registered reader of one store: a name, a type, a
.B retaining
flag and a durable position.
Two types today \(em
.BR timber\-otlp (1)
ships ENTRIES over OTLP, and
.B frames
ships CHUNKS verbatim over the native wire (see REPLICATION) \(em and any
shipper the registry learns to exec is one tomorrow. The registry is one
directory per follower:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/var/lib/timberfs/followers/<name>/
    follower.json   store, type, retaining, config  (the operator writes)
    cursor.json     seq, n, delivered               (the follower writes)
    follower.lock   held while it runs              (`run` acquires)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The declaration and the position are separate files because they have
separate OWNERS: a position save is a whole-file replace that
deliberately drops keys it does not own, so one file would make every
position write preserve operator fields, and would race
.BR update .
The lock is a third file because a position save replaces its inode by
rename, and a lock on a renamed-over inode silently stops excluding
anyone.
.PP
This is a Postgres replication slot, and the parallel holds all the way
down: an operator-chosen name unique per host while the registration
itself records which store it belongs to, and an unused registration
pinning data forever \(em with a size budget as the backstop, exactly as
.B max_slot_wal_keep_size
is. timberfs remains a log with interest-based truncation, not a work
queue: position-based and at-least-once, with no per-entry ack, no
redelivery and no dead-letter.
.PP
.B The follower declares its store, by identity.
Flat names mean the relation is recorded ONCE, by the party that knows
it, so a store keeps no follower list and there is no reverse index to
fall out of sync. The identity is the store's
.B .bark
.IR id ,
minted by
.B follower create
when the store has none: a path would not do, a store being movable, and
a path can come to hold a different store.
.PP
.B Why a registered object and not a cursor found by convention.
A directory of cursors cannot express INTENT \(em an ad-hoc
.B \-\-cursor
left behind in it would pin the store, so a reader would change
retention by leaving a file around. It cannot tell
.RI \(dq "nobody has ever read this" \(dq
from
.RI \(dq "the consumer's file was deleted" \(dq
\(em both are zero cursors, and the first is exactly the follower
deployed before it first runs. And deriving a cursor's path from a
program name, which is what made the convention convenient, COLLIDES the
moment two shippers of one type follow one store: last writer wins, one
advances past data the other never sent.
.PP
.B timberfs list
carries a
.B FOLLOWERS
column and
.B timberfs info
a
.B followers
block. Per follower:
.B how far behind
on the write axis
.RI ( "at the live edge"
when nothing newer than its position exists \(em a follower keeping up
with a live store always sits INSIDE the newest chunk, never past it),
.B how much it is holding
(compressed bytes from its own chunk to the end of the store),
.B how much it has delivered
in total, and whether it is
.BR running .
The store-level number is the furthest-behind RETAINING follower's
backlog \(em or the whole store, when one of them has never run, since
that one holds everything.
.PP
.B GAP
is the one alarming state. Retention acts on the head of a store, so a
follower down longer than the store's
.B retain
window has its position dropped out from under it; it then resumes at
whatever is now oldest, and the chunks in between are gone \(em reported
as an exact COUNT, the chunk numbers stating how many rather than a
timestamp comparison implying a duration.
.BR timber\-otlp (1)
warns by name on resume with the size of the hole, and this view shows
the same fact from the store's side, before it becomes loss. A
.B frames
follower learns it from the other end instead: the receiver's answer says
what it holds, so a gap is the distance between that and this store's
oldest chunk. What to
watch is a follower whose lag approaches the retention window \(em that
is the disconnection budget being spent.
.PP
.B The lock never gates retention.
A follower that is temporarily down holds no lock and, once
.B retaining
is honoured, must still pin the head \(em that is the entire purpose of a
spool. The lock detects collisions and reports liveness; it decides
nothing about what may be dropped. Inverting that would turn
.RI \(dq "the shipper is down" \(dq
into
.RI \(dq "drop everything it had not read" \(dq.
.PP
.B The cursors key is superseded.
Before the registry a store could DECLARE a directory to look in \(em
.B timberfs set backing/app.log cursors=/var/lib/timberfs
\(em and every cursor there naming that store was reported as a
consumer. A follower declares its store, so a store declares nothing;
the key is still honoured, and reported as superseded wherever it is
found. Keep it only for an unregistered read-only tap, and expect it to
go.
.PP
.B What retaining costs the store.
Nothing, until the store asks for it. The flag is one half of a pair: a
follower declares that it retains, and the STORE declares
.B retain_unconsumed
(see
.BR RETENTION ).
Either alone holds nothing back, and
.B follower status
says which case it is looking at rather than letting a declared interest
read as an enforced one.
.SH REPLICATION
Two wires carry a store to another host, and they answer different
questions.
.B ENTRIES
over OTLP
.RB ( timber\-otlp (1),
a
.B \-\-type otlp
follower) go to anything that speaks the protocol, and can be filtered,
merged and re-windowed on the way.
.B FRAMES
over the native wire
.RB ( frames\-send ,
a
.B \-\-type frames
follower) go only to another timberfs, and go VERBATIM: the compressed
frames are copied, so nothing is decompressed at either end and the
destination's
.I .trunk
is byte-identical to the source's. Measured on 200k apache entries, the
bytes on the wire are a wash against gzipped OTLP while the CPU is not
\(em and that work lands on the machine serving production traffic.
.PP
.B A replica or a copy, and the difference is the numbering.
With
.B \-\-replica
the destination keeps the sender's chunk numbers and records its
.IR origin_id ,
so
.RI ( origin_id ", " seq )
names the same bytes at both ends. That is refused unless the numbering
continues EXACTLY: a preserved number with a gap under it is an address
that lies, so a stream beginning mid-tape says so rather than leaving one.
Without it the destination renumbers and claims no origin \(em always
possible, and weaker, since neither the address nor the evidence of a gap
survives. The two travel together or not at all.
.PP
.B Identity is the destination's own.
It mints its own
.IR id ,
records the sender as
.IR derived_from ,
and takes the sender's LABELS \(em while retention, the index and the wal
stay its own policy. Settings never travel.
.PP
.B One destination store, one origin.
A second origin into a store that already holds one is refused, naming
both. Without that a host reinstall, or two hosts sharing a short
hostname, silently appends a second tape to the first while the manifest
describes only one of them.
.PP
.B The handshake is what this has over a pipe.
A sender opens by saying what it is about to send; the receiver answers
with what it ALREADY HOLDS, so the sender resumes from the receiver's
position and cannot re-send \(em running
.B frames\-send
twice does nothing and says so. Or the receiver answers with a conflict,
naming the origin that holds the name, so a collision is a sentence on a
terminal at setup rather than a mislabelled store found weeks later.
.PP
.B What it cannot do.
Only SEALED chunks exist to ship, so a replica trails the source by one
chunk flush; the live edge belongs to
.B query \-\-follow
on the source itself. And frames are 1:1 \(em opaque bytes whose rings
must stay sorted, so interleaving two sources into one store needs
decoding, which is the entries path's job. Frames replicate; records merge
and transform.
.SH FORESTS
A
.I forest
is a directory
.B timberfs
searches for stores by a short
.IR handle ,
so
.B timberfs query nginx
finds
.I /var/log/timberfs/nginx/nginx.log
without spelling out the path. Handle lookup applies to the source
argument of
.BR query ", " info ", " index ", " reindex ", " set ", " rotate " and " export ;
a full path, a relative path or a
.B .timber
bundle is always taken literally, so every existing invocation is
unchanged \(em a forest is consulted only for a bare token (no
.BR / )
that names no store on disk.
.PP
Forests are declared with
.B timberfs forest create
(and listed with
.BR "timberfs forest list" ),
which writes the configuration described next \(em so the file is what
gets read, not what has to be written.
.PP
That configuration is
.IR /etc/timberfs/forests.d/*.conf ,
one forest per file, the same
.B KEY=VALUE
idiom as an instance
.IR .conf ,
read in sorted filename order. The only key is
.BR DIR ,
an absolute directory; blank lines,
.B #
comments and unknown keys are ignored, and a configured directory that
does not exist is skipped. The package ships
.I default.conf
with
.BR DIR=/var/log/timberfs ;
edit it, drop in another
.IR .conf ,
or delete it to disable handle lookup (it is a conffile, so edits
survive upgrades).
.PP
A store's handle is its
.B .rings
file name minus
.B .rings
and a single trailing
.BR .log ,
so a flat
.I nginx.rings
and a nested
.I nginx/nginx.log.rings
both resolve as
.BR nginx ", while " metrics.jsonl.rings " keeps its " .jsonl .
Each forest's root and its immediate subdirectories are scanned. A
handle that matches no store, or more than one, is an error that names
what was searched and asks for a full path.
.PP
Two environment variables override this.
.B TIMBERFS_FORESTS
(colon-separated absolute directories) replaces the configuration
entirely, so a process with it set cannot see a declared forest at all \(em
.B forest create
warns when it is set, because otherwise the write succeeds and
.B forest list
does not show it.
.B TIMBERFS_FORESTS_DIR
moves where the declarations are read from and written to, for a test or
an install that is not rooted at
.IR / .
.SH WHERE AN INTAKE WRITES
The four intakes \(em
.BR forward\-intake ", " otlp\-intake ", " incus\-intake " and " frames\-intake
\(em create stores as data arrives, so each one has to be told where. That
is a
.B forest:
.PP
.nf
timberfs otlp\-intake \-\-forest default
.fi
.PP
.B \-\-forest
takes a name, as
.B timberfs forest list
gives it, and an unknown one is an error that lists what IS declared \(em a
forest name is a small closed set an operator chose, so the answer to a
typo is the set itself.
.PP
.B \-\-into\-dir
.I DIR
is the older form and is
.BR DEPRECATED :
it still works, and it warns. It remains the only way to write into a
directory that is
.B not
a forest. Giving both is refused rather than one being picked.
.SS A confined intake gets a SIBLING forest
An intake with
.B \-\-auto\-create
needs write permission on wherever it writes, so a confined instance (a
.B User=
drop-in) is better given a forest of its own than write permission on the
shared one. That forest is a
.BR sibling ,
not a subdirectory:
.PP
.nf
timberfs forest create /srv/otel\-logs
timberfs otlp\-intake \-\-forest otel\-logs
.fi
.PP
Forests may not
.B nest
\(em a forest is scanned at its root and one directory deep, so a store
inside two of them has a handle that resolves to neither \(em and
.B forest create
refuses one that overlaps another. A subdirectory of an existing forest is
therefore not a forest, and reaching it needs the deprecated
.BR \-\-into\-dir .
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
The package ships completion scripts for
.B bash
and
.BR zsh ,
installed to the standard vendor locations
.RI ( /usr/share/bash\-completion/completions/timberfs " and "
.IR /usr/share/zsh/vendor\-completions/_timberfs )
so both shells load them automatically, with no per-user setup.
.B timberfs
.RB [ TAB ]
lists the subcommands; the store argument of
.BR query ", " info ", " index ", " reindex ", " set " and the source of "
.BR rotate " and " export
additionally completes bare
.B FORESTS
handles, gathered live from
.B timberfs list \-\-names
and offered alongside normal file-path completion. With no forests
configured (or if that call errors), completion silently falls back to
file paths \(em never an error at the prompt.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress informational notes on standard error \(em scan reports,
progress, stitch plans, summaries. Errors and warnings still print.
Accepted by every subcommand. (Long-only on purpose:
.B \-q
keeps its grep(1) meaning available.)
.SH THE TWO CLOCKS
Every entry effectively has two timestamps: the one its line CARRIES
(what happened when \(em the one you can see) and the WRITE time it
arrived in the store (the one the index searches). For imported data
they are identical; for live data they differ by buffering. By default,
.B query
and
.B grep
select chunks by the write-time index (widened by about a minute to
catch buffered stragglers), then verify every entry against
.BR \-\-from / \-\-to
by the timestamp its own line carries \(em so the output answers the
question you asked, in the timestamps you can see. Entries whose
timestamps cannot be read are always included, never hidden; a store
with no parseable timestamps at all falls back to the raw, unwidened
write-time window with a note.
.B \-\-show\-write\-time
annotates each entry with the write window it arrived in \(em and, when
its own stamp falls outside that window, the offset \(em making any
divergence visible and explainable.
.B \-\-by\-write\-time
is the raw escape hatch: chunk output selected by write time only, no
parsing (the pre-0.7.4 behavior).
.B \-0
.RB ( \-\-null )
emits NUL-terminated entry RECORDS \(em a multiline entry (stack trace)
stays one record \(em for
.BR "xargs \-0" ,
.BR "sort \-z" ,
.B "uniq \-z"
pipelines.
.B \-\-records
emits the typed record stream for timber-aware consumers: the same
NUL-framed entries interleaved with metadata records carrying the
format version, an echo of the selection, per-source index statistics,
each entry's length, own timestamp and write window, and end-of-stream
totals whose presence proves the stream arrived complete \(em see
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
for the grammar. Plain
.B \-0
stays the choice for ordinary Unix tools. Persistent whole-hour offsets between the two clocks are
detected and warned about once (on one host the clocks cancel, so such
an offset is a timezone or format misconfiguration, not clock skew).
Declare exotic line formats once in the manifest
.RB ( timestamp_regex ,
.BR timestamp_format ,
.B timestamp_utc
via
.B timberfs set
\(em or import's flags, which persist the declaration); they describe
the CONTENT, so unlike settings they inherit into derived artifacts.
Do not backfill historical data through
.B append
(its index gets today's write times): import the file instead.
.SH TIME FORMATS
.I TIME
arguments accept RFC 3339
.RB ( 2026\-07\-10T13:42:00+02:00 ),
.BR "YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM" [ :SS ]
(local time; a
.B T
separator also works, and dots as date separators \(em paste straight
from logback-style logs), a bare
.B YYYY\-MM\-DD
(midnight local time, so
.B \-\-from 2026\-07\-10 \-\-to 2026\-07\-11
selects exactly that day),
.BR HH:MM [ :SS ]
(today, local time), and unix epoch seconds or milliseconds.
.SH ON-DISK FORMAT
Each logical file
.I name
is backed by two files in the backing directory:
.TP
.IB name .trunk
The data: a plain concatenation of zstd frames, one per chunk, with no
wrapper bytes. The full uncompressed content is therefore always
recoverable with stock tools:
.B zstd \-dc
.IB name .trunk
.TP
.IB name .rings
The index: a 64-byte header (the magic
.RB \(lq RING0002 \(rq
, the header's own length, an incompatible-feature mask, the chunk-number
high-water mark, and reserved zeroes) followed by fixed-size 56-byte
records (little-endian u64 fields: uncompressed start/length, compressed
start/length, first/last write time in unix milliseconds, and the chunk
NUMBER), appended in write order. The number is assigned at append, never
reused, and preserved verbatim by a head-drop, so it is NOT the record's
position \(em it is what a consumer's cursor holds, the write axis being
only mostly sorted (see THE TWO CLOCKS). It is local to one store: a chunk
copied into another is renumbered there. The previous layout
.RB \(lq RING0001 \(rq
(8-byte header, 48-byte records, no number) is still read, and a writer
migrates a store to the current layout when it opens it. A reader takes
record 0's offset from the length the file declares, not from its own
build's, so a later version may lengthen the header; a feature bit it does
not recognise makes it refuse rather than read the records wrongly.
.PP
Chunks are written data-first, index-second; on open, index records
pointing past the end of the data are dropped and orphaned data bytes are
overwritten.
.BR fsync (2)
through the mount flushes the buffer as a chunk and syncs both backing
files.
.PP
When
.B wal
is declared (see
.BR create ),
a third file,
.IB name .sap ,
holds every appended entry raw (u32 length, u64 write-from, u64
write-to, the payload, a trailing crc32), fsynced once a second \(em the
durability point, independent of the chunk flush schedule. It holds the
same bytes as the in-memory buffer by construction; a mid-flush crash is
resolved by comparing the sidecar's recorded
.I base
(the trunk size when it was created) against the trunk's current size.
A writer reopening the store after a crash replays it. A
.B "query \-\-follow"
reads it as it grows, which is what puts an entry in front of an operator
before its chunk exists: a segment's content is exactly the next chunk's
bytes, so a follower drops from that chunk the bytes it already served
and can neither gap nor repeat. The seal is a rename, so a new inode
marks the generation; only the CRC-valid prefix is ever read.
.PP
The daemon holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on
.IB backingdir /.timberfs.lock
recording its mountpoint; offline rotation takes the same lock.
.SH EXTENDED ATTRIBUTES
Files in the mount expose read-only metadata via
.BR getfattr (1):
.BR user.timberfs.chunks ,
.BR user.timberfs.compressed_size ,
.BR user.timberfs.first_write ,
.BR user.timberfs.last_write .
The attribute
.B user.timberfs.rotate
is a write-only control interface used internally by
.BR "timberfs rotate" .
.SH SYSTEMD
The Debian package ships a template unit
.BR timberfs@.service :
create
.IB /etc/timberfs/ instance .conf
defining
.BR BACKING ,
.B MOUNTPOINT
and optionally
.BR EXTRA_OPTS ,
then
.B systemctl enable \-\-now
.BI timberfs@ instance .
Stopping the unit unmounts first, so the daemon flushes all buffers and
exits cleanly. An example configuration is installed under
.IR /usr/share/doc/timberfs/examples/ .
.PP
Every intake below writes the same layout,
.IB /var/log/timberfs/ name / name .log\fR:
one directory per store, named after the store, which is also the handle
.B timberfs query
resolves it by (see
.BR FORESTS ).
A store's path therefore says what the store IS and nothing about how the
data reached it \(em the same log keeps its name and its store when it is
later fed by another route. The directory is per store because a directory
is what a writer needs permission on, so one store can be given one owner.
Point an intake's
.B STORE_DIR
or
.B \-\-forest
somewhere else to group stores by subject, one owner and one place per
group. What stays intake\-specific is intake configuration \(em
.IB /etc/timberfs/text- instance .conf\fR,
or the FIFO under
.I /run/timberfs
\(em because that belongs to the mechanism, not to the log.
.PP
A socket\-activated log\-intake pair,
.B timberfs\-log@.socket
and
.BR timberfs\-log@.service ,
streams a producer's
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream into a store over a FIFO.
.PP
Its plain\-text sibling,
.B timberfs\-text@.socket
and
.BR timberfs\-text@.service ,
does the same for a producer that can only log to a PATH \(em Apache's
.B CustomLog
and
.BR ErrorLog ,
nginx's
.BR access_log :
the producer writes
.IB /run/timberfs/text/ instance .pipe
and the store lands in
.IB /var/log/timberfs/ instance / instance .log\fR.
The point of that route for such a producer is that the writer is NOT its
child, so a reload never spawns, races or kills it \(em and because the
socket holds the FIFO open
.BR O_RDWR ,
lines written while the writer is restarting or being upgraded wait in the
kernel pipe instead of being lost. Retention and the token index are
configuration:
.I /etc/timberfs/text.conf
sets
.B DECLARE
for every instance and
.IB /etc/timberfs/text- instance .conf
overrides it for one, and both are applied to the store on every start.
ONE instance can take every vhost of a web server \(em put the vhost in the
log format
.RB ( %v ),
and a new site needs no logging configuration at all \(em or a site can have
its own instance when it needs its own retention or owner; the two layouts
are the same two units.
.PP
.B timberfs\-follow@.service
takes the other route for such a producer: it leaves the file alone and
reads it
.RB ( "import \-\-follow" ).
Each instance reads
.IB /etc/timberfs/follow- instance .conf
for
.B SOURCE
(required) and optionally
.BR STORE_DIR ,
.B DECLARE
and
.BR EXTRA_OPTS ,
then
.B systemctl enable \-\-now
.BI timberfs\-follow@ instance \fR.
Nothing in the producer's write path depends on it \(em a follower that
stalls or dies can only fall behind, where a FIFO this side stops draining
blocks the producer (and ends the process, for Exim). That makes it the
safer default, at the cost of the plain file continuing to exist.
.PP
A fourth, socket\-activated pair,
.B timberfs\-forward.socket
and
.BR timberfs\-forward.service ,
runs
.B forward\-intake
listening on TCP for Fluentd Forward protocol v1 senders (Docker's
.B fluentd
log driver, Fluent Bit, Fluentd). Not templated: Forward multiplexes every
tag over the one connection, so one instance serves every tag.
.PP
A fifth, socket\-activated pair,
.B timberfs\-otlp.socket
and
.BR timberfs\-otlp.service ,
runs
.B otlp\-intake
listening on TCP for OTLP/HTTP senders (any OpenTelemetry SDK or
Collector). Also not templated \(em OTLP multiplexes resources inside the
request body, and each stream lands in its own store.
.PP
A sixth pair,
.B timberfs\-frames.socket
and
.BR timberfs\-frames.service ,
runs
.B frames\-intake
for the NATIVE replication wire: compressed chunks land verbatim, so a
store received here is byte-identical to its source, its
.I .grain
included. The
other three intakes take data from other SYSTEMS and compress it; this one
takes it from another timberfs and copies what is already compressed \(em
which is the axis to choose on. The shipped unit passes
.B \-\-replica
and deliberately not
.BR \-\-auto\-create ,
and its socket is on loopback: an archive taking senders from other hosts
overrides that, and there is no TLS here. See REPLICATION.
.PP
Finally, the template unit
.B timberfs\-otlp@.service
runs
.BR timber\-otlp (1)
in the other direction, shipping ONE store to an OTLP/HTTP receiver:
create
.IB /etc/timberfs/otlp- instance .conf
defining
.BR STORE ,
.B ENDPOINT
and optionally
.BR EXTRA_OPTS ,
then
.B systemctl enable \-\-now
.BI timberfs\-otlp@ instance \fR.
One instance per store \(em the cursor is a position in one store, and a
stalled receiver must not hold up an unrelated one. Its cursor lives in
.IR /var/lib/timberfs/ .
.PP
.B timberfs\-follower@.service
is the registry's version of that, and the one to prefer: declare the
follower once with
.B timberfs follower create
and the unit needs no per-instance
.I .conf
at all, because the store, the type and the endpoint are in the
declaration.
.B ExecStart
is
.BR "timberfs follower run %i" ,
which reads that declaration and EXECs the right shipper \(em a
dispatcher, not a supervisor, so systemd keeps the lifecycle. Its
.B StateDirectory=
is
.IB /var/lib/timberfs/followers/ instance \fR,
holding the declaration, the position and the lock;
.I those permissions decide what a store's retention may drop,
so treat the directory as privileged. An optional
.IB /etc/timberfs/follower- instance .conf
exists only for SECRETS \(em the registry is world-readable so that
.B follower list
and
.B info
work without write access, which is no place for a bearer token.
.PP
.B Not to be confused with
.B timberfs\-follow@.service
\(em one letter, opposite direction. That one is an INTAKE
.RB ( "timberfs import \-\-follow" ):
it reads a producer's file INTO a store. This one reads a store OUT to
somewhere else.
.PP
The full directory layout, all eight unit families, the ownership model and
self\-restart on upgrade are documented in
.IR /usr/share/doc/timberfs/deployment.md .
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount and use:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs mount ./logs\-backing ./logs &
myapp >> logs/app.log
tail \-f logs/app.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
Or skip FUSE entirely and pipe:
.PP
.nf
.RS
myapp 2>&1 | timberfs append \-\-into logs\-backing/app.log
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract a time window from a huge log, then trim with grep:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs query logs\-backing/app.log \-\-from 13:42 \-\-to 13:43 | grep ERROR
.RE
.fi
.PP
Daily rotation and 30-day retention, no recompression:
.PP
.nf
.RS
timberfs rotate logs\-backing/app.log app\-$(date \-d yesterday +%F).log \\
    \-\-cutoff "$(date +%F) 00:00"
timberfs rotate logs\-backing/app.log \-\-delete \\
    \-\-cutoff "$(date \-d '30 days ago' +%F) 00:00"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Disaster recovery with stock tools only:
.PP
.nf
.RS
zstd \-dc logs\-backing/app.log.trunk > app.log.recovered
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero on any error (2 for command-line usage errors).
